SQL Databases store data in strictly typed tables with rows & columns.

Relationships are formed by linking rows across tables using Keys.

Primary Key (PK): A unique identifier for a specific row in a table (usually an ID, auto-incrementing integer, or UUID).

Foreign Key (FK): A column in Table B that references the Primary Key of Table A. This creates the link between data.

There are 3 ways tables talk to each other:
- One-to-many(1:N)
  - Ex: One user has many chat messages
  - Implementation: messages table has a user_id foreign key.
- Many-to-Many(N:M)
  - Ex: One Game has many Tags, and one Tag belongs to many Games
  -Implementation: Created using a third "Junction Table" (e.g. game_tags with game_id & tag.id).
- One-to-One(1:1)
  - Ex: One User has one Game Developer Profile
  - Implementation: developer_profiles table has a user_id foreign key marked as UNIQUE.

Basic SQL Schema Syntax:

// Create Users Table
CREATE TABLE users (
  id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
  username VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL UNIQUE,
  email VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL UNIQUE,
  created_at TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
);

// Create the Chat Messages Table with a Foreign Key link
CREATE TABLE chat_messages (
  id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
  user_id UUID NOT NULL,
  room_name VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL DEFAULT 'general',
  message_text TEXT NOT NULL,
  created_at TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,

  CONSTRAINT fk_user
    FOREIGN KEY(user_id)
    REFERENCES users(id)
    ON DELETE CASCADE
);


What does "ON DELETE CASCADE" do?
If a user deletes their account from the users table, SQL will automatically clean up and delete all of their messages in chat_messages so you don't end up with orphaned data.

Fundamental SQL Queries(CRUD)

Create:

INSERT INTO users (username, email)
VALUES ('kayfowan', 'kai@example.com');

READ:

SELECT id, username FROM users WHERE username = 'kayfowan';

UPDATE:

UPDATE users
SET username = 'kayfowan_dev'
WHERE email = 'kai@example.com';

DELETE:

DELETE FROM users WHERE id = 'some-uuid-here';








